1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,710
Think about a notice, set of directions,
or supply list

2
00:00:03,710 --> 00:00:06,710
that was easy to use. What helped you find
the

3
00:00:06,710 --> 00:00:10,430
next step? We will apply those same
practical standards to

4
00:00:10,430 --> 00:00:14,470
a small resource pack: a handout, a supply
workbook, and

5
00:00:14,470 --> 00:00:18,210
three slides. Choose a fictional community
activity such as a

6
00:00:18,210 --> 00:00:21,950
library computer-help session or a
neighborhood meeting. Decide

7
00:00:21,950 --> 00:00:24,170
who will use the material and what they
need to

8
00:00:24,170 --> 00:00:27,290
do after reading it. A beautiful page that
leaves the

9
00:00:27,290 --> 00:00:31,270
reader unsure is unfinished. Keep that
reader's task in view

10
00:00:31,270 --> 00:00:35,790
as we make choices about words, structure,
calculations, pictures,

11
00:00:36,290 --> 00:00:37,570
and the final file format.

12
00:00:39,431 --> 00:00:41,871
Start the handout with the service and the
next action.

13
00:00:42,351 --> 00:00:44,671
Use a real heading style for the title and
section

14
00:00:44,671 --> 00:00:48,071
headings, followed by short paragraphs or
a numbered list when

15
00:00:48,071 --> 00:00:52,491
order matters. Selecting bold text changes
appearance, but does

16
00:00:52,491 --> 00:00:56,371
not necessarily identify a heading to
assistive technology or a

17
00:00:56,371 --> 00:01:00,551
navigation pane. A numbered sequence might
say choose a task,

18
00:01:01,351 --> 00:01:04,211
visit the learning desk, and ask how to
repeat the

19
00:01:04,211 --> 00:01:07,371
skill at home. Use link text that names
the destination

20
00:01:07,371 --> 00:01:11,251
instead of click here. Read the document
in order without

21
00:01:11,251 --> 00:01:14,731
relying on color or position alone. Does
it still make

22
00:01:14,731 --> 00:01:17,771
sense? That is a useful first
accessibility check.

23
00:01:19,526 --> 00:01:22,526
Select a short piece of text before
changing it, and

24
00:01:22,526 --> 00:01:26,506
check what the selection includes. Common
shortcuts such as copy,

25
00:01:26,806 --> 00:01:30,906
paste, save, and undo can reduce repeated
mouse work; your

26
00:01:30,906 --> 00:01:34,326
instructor can show the keys for your
computer. Practice on

27
00:01:34,326 --> 00:01:39,266
a copy so a mistake is easy to reverse.
When

28
00:01:39,266 --> 00:01:43,226
reviewing someone else's document, a
comment asks a question or

29
00:01:43,226 --> 00:01:47,686
suggests an improvement. Tracked changes
record proposed edits for

30
00:01:47,686 --> 00:01:51,166
review, where the application supports
them. They are not the

31
00:01:51,166 --> 00:01:55,566
same as silently replacing the text. Pause
and propose one

32
00:01:55,566 --> 00:01:59,446
specific improvement to the handout.
Explain the reader's problem

33
00:01:59,446 --> 00:02:02,826
it solves, then let the author decide
whether to accept it.

34
00:02:04,590 --> 00:02:08,430
A workbook can contain several worksheets.
Within a sheet, columns

35
00:02:08,430 --> 00:02:11,830
usually have letters, rows have numbers,
and a cell address

36
00:02:11,830 --> 00:02:16,370
combines both. B2 means column B, row two.
Our fictional

37
00:02:16,370 --> 00:02:19,470
supply list puts item names in column A
and dollar

38
00:02:19,470 --> 00:02:23,290
amounts in column B: paper at twelve,
folders at eight,

39
00:02:23,290 --> 00:02:25,990
and pens at five. Include a heading that
explains the

40
00:02:25,990 --> 00:02:29,770
units, so twelve is clearly dollars rather
than boxes. Enter

41
00:02:29,770 --> 00:02:32,270
the values in separate cells instead of
putting the whole

42
00:02:32,270 --> 00:02:35,970
list into one cell. Before calculating
anything, check that each

43
00:02:35,970 --> 00:02:39,610
amount sits beside the intended item. A
correct formula cannot

44
00:02:39,610 --> 00:02:41,690
rescue numbers assigned to the wrong rows.

45
00:02:43,640 --> 00:02:47,340
Select a total cell outside the three
input cells and

46
00:02:47,340 --> 00:02:52,460
enter equals SUM, followed by the range B2
through B4

47
00:02:52,460 --> 00:02:57,160
in parentheses. The colon means include
the cells from the

48
00:02:57,160 --> 00:03:01,820
first address through the last. The
expected total is twenty-five

49
00:03:01,820 --> 00:03:06,780
dollars. Now change paper from twelve to
fifteen. The total

50
00:03:06,780 --> 00:03:10,200
should become twenty-eight because the
formula refers to the cells,

51
00:03:10,720 --> 00:03:14,020
not to a permanently typed answer. If it
stays at

52
00:03:14,020 --> 00:03:18,280
twenty-five, inspect the formula and
range. Did you type the

53
00:03:18,280 --> 00:03:22,840
total manually? Did the range omit a cell?
Pause and

54
00:03:22,840 --> 00:03:26,640
predict the result before changing a
value. Then compare the

55
00:03:26,640 --> 00:03:29,780
observed total with your prediction and
correct any mismatch.

56
00:03:31,909 --> 00:03:34,569
A chart should help someone see a
relationship in the

57
00:03:34,569 --> 00:03:38,909
checked data. To compare the three supply
costs, select the

58
00:03:38,909 --> 00:03:42,509
item labels and their values, then choose
a simple bar

59
00:03:42,509 --> 00:03:45,909
or column chart. Give it a meaningful
title and make

60
00:03:45,909 --> 00:03:49,469
the dollar units clear. Check that the
labels still match

61
00:03:49,469 --> 00:03:53,229
their bars. A large bar should represent
the larger value,

62
00:03:53,549 --> 00:03:57,149
not merely a brighter color. After
changing the paper cost,

63
00:03:57,509 --> 00:04:00,829
confirm that the chart follows the updated
cells. If a

64
00:04:00,829 --> 00:04:04,309
chart looks surprising, return to the
table before decorating it.

65
00:04:04,889 --> 00:04:07,909
Keep the table available so a reader can
inspect exact

66
00:04:07,909 --> 00:04:12,349
amounts and understand the information
without relying only on the graphic.

67
00:04:14,256 --> 00:04:17,576
For a three-slide presentation, use the
first slide to name

68
00:04:17,576 --> 00:04:20,936
the useful task, the second to demonstrate
the key steps,

69
00:04:21,516 --> 00:04:24,916
and the third to show the next action.
Prefer a

70
00:04:24,916 --> 00:04:28,176
relevant picture or diagram to a paragraph
copied from the

71
00:04:28,176 --> 00:04:34,176
handout. Keep text large and explain
unfamiliar terms aloud. Cropping

72
00:04:34,176 --> 00:04:37,496
removes outer parts of an image from the
view; resizing

73
00:04:37,496 --> 00:04:41,876
changes its displayed dimensions. Those
actions solve different

74
00:04:41,876 --> 00:04:46,196
problems. Preserve the original file
before experimenting, and check

75
00:04:46,196 --> 00:04:48,996
that a crop has not removed information
the viewer needs.

76
00:04:49,916 --> 00:04:53,756
If an image carries meaning, provide an
equivalent explanation or

77
00:04:53,756 --> 00:04:56,116
alternative text in the format you are
using.

78
00:04:57,881 --> 00:05:01,521
A useful instructional clip has a clear
beginning, an understandable

79
00:05:01,521 --> 00:05:06,401
action, and a visible result. Trimming
removes unwanted material

80
00:05:06,401 --> 00:05:09,841
at the start or end. Splitting allows you
to work

81
00:05:09,841 --> 00:05:12,941
with sections inside a clip, such as
removing a long

82
00:05:12,941 --> 00:05:17,461
unrelated interruption. Watch both sides
of an edit: did it

83
00:05:17,461 --> 00:05:20,141
cut off the first syllable or remove the
step that

84
00:05:20,141 --> 00:05:24,601
explains the result? Keep speech
intelligible and check captions

85
00:05:24,601 --> 00:05:28,341
against the final audio after editing.
Captions from an earlier

86
00:05:28,341 --> 00:05:32,561
version may drift. You do not need
elaborate effects. The

87
00:05:32,561 --> 00:05:35,441
goal is for another person to follow the
action, pause

88
00:05:35,441 --> 00:05:37,961
when needed, and understand what changed.

89
00:05:39,903 --> 00:05:43,503
Decide what the next person needs to do.
An editable

90
00:05:43,503 --> 00:05:47,843
document supports revision. A PDF often
preserves a finished layout

91
00:05:47,843 --> 00:05:51,963
for viewing or printing. A CSV carries
plain table data,

92
00:05:51,963 --> 00:05:55,303
but does not preserve a workbook's full
formatting and formula

93
00:05:55,303 --> 00:05:59,063
behavior. Keep the editable original as
well as the export.

94
00:05:59,943 --> 00:06:03,543
Reopen the exported file and inspect the
page breaks, labels,

95
00:06:04,143 --> 00:06:08,263
links, and important values. If you reused
someone else's image

96
00:06:08,263 --> 00:06:11,443
or wording, check the permission or
license and provide the

97
00:06:11,443 --> 00:06:14,823
required credit. Finding an image in a
search result does

98
00:06:14,823 --> 00:06:18,503
not automatically give you permission to
reuse it. Use original

99
00:06:18,503 --> 00:06:21,903
or appropriately licensed material for the
practice pack.

100
00:06:23,690 --> 00:06:26,330
Pause and ask a partner to use your
resource pack

101
00:06:26,330 --> 00:06:30,990
without your explanation first. Can they
identify the next action

102
00:06:30,990 --> 00:06:34,350
on the handout? Can they find the total
and explain

103
00:06:34,350 --> 00:06:38,950
what changed when one cost changed? Can
they tell what

104
00:06:38,950 --> 00:06:43,950
each slide contributes? Record one point
of confusion and revise

105
00:06:43,950 --> 00:06:48,490
it. Then reopen the export and repeat the
affected check.

106
00:06:49,310 --> 00:06:52,790
This is useful evidence of learning: the
document has structure,

107
00:06:53,590 --> 00:06:57,530
the calculation responds correctly, and
another reader can act on

108
00:06:57,530 --> 00:07:01,410
the result. Carry the same routine into a
household budget,

109
00:07:01,730 --> 00:07:06,050
a community notice, or another task you
choose. Create for

110
00:07:06,050 --> 00:07:10,110
a reader, test the result, and keep a
recoverable original.
